<!--Post: 12214--><!--Time: 1111778778-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12214"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lostdog<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lostdog<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 25, 2005 - 11:26 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Do not toot. <BR> <BR>Bloooooooowwwww your horn Brother Huff! <BR> <BR>In my salad days, I worked as a professional magician. <BR>I’ve studied the craft of conjuring since childhood. <BR>Mentalism is a venerable offshoot of the magic arts. <BR>It had its heyday in the vaudeville era and gave rise to supper club mindreading acts in the 50s and 60s.  <BR>Remember the Amazing Kreskin? <BR> <BR>Using trickery to feign psychic powers is as old as any religion on earth. <BR> <BR>Skull up on the classic techniques of soothsayers and your skepticism will overflow. <BR> <BR>In the hands of a skilled &#40;and ethical&#41; mentalist, a good time will be had by all and joyous amazement will ensue. <BR> <BR>On the other hand, the “psychic friend” is a special kind of sociopath. <BR> <BR>Harry Houdini, a formidable genius, spent years and thousands of dollars in pursuit of the “one” who could connect him with his beloved, departed mother. <BR>Time after time, he found he had fallen victim to the same parlor tricks that spawned his own brilliant career. <BR> <BR>Eventually, he reinvented his stage act to expose the manipulations of “psychic” charlatans to the public. <BR> <BR>Does this begin to prove that the ability to see beyond the physical world is an impossibility? <BR> <BR>No way. <BR> <BR>Even the great Houdini or Stephen Hawking or Ray Charles only utilized a teeny tiny portion of their gray matter to achieve mastery. <BR> <BR>Mystery, however, lies in the uncharted corners of human consciousness. <BR> <BR>I believe you were in that misty hall of mirrors Steve.  <BR>It’s a treacherous place, isn’t it? <BR>As soon as you begin to trust…BAM – you walk smack in to a wall of glass. <BR>Is that your reflection – or someone else? <BR>What was it that led you there I the first place? <BR>Stress? Dream state? Subconscious memory fart? Pure uninterrupted thought? <BR> <BR>Go figure. <BR> <BR>Humankind seems as far from harnessing that power as ever before. <BR>Come to think of it, could be that we’re farther from it than our ancestors. <BR>Life is just too noisy today. <BR>I heard about a study of monks who devote their waking hours to meditation. <BR>Research on these cats showed measurable levels of increased consciousness. <BR> <BR>When you can snatch the pebble from my hand… it will be time for you to know. <BR> <BR>The point is, you trusted your visions, hunches and deductions enough to publish them. <BR>O.K. not as Steve Huff, but in the safer guise of your fictionites. <BR>Still, as Steve Huff &#40;if that is your real name!&#41; you committed to some very honed and on target portrayals. <BR> <BR>So BLOW that mofokin’ horn bro. PLAY YOUR INSTRUMENT! Take another chorus. <BR> <BR>I’ll dig whatever you got to say. <BR> <BR>I’ll dig it because I love to ponder Rader’s discovery of your posts. <BR>I can only imagine the rain of flop sweat that drenched him as he read your profile... <BR>and glanced at his reflection in the darkened “clean room” window. <BR> <BR>“Who in Christ’s name is on the other side of that glass! <BR> <BR>Who indeed? <BR> <BR>Lrry’sDog   <BR> <BR>    <BR> <!--/Text--></td></tr>
