<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12236"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 25, 2005 - 2:45 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Well, dang. I feel like I owe LD some money for that one...and I am not in the least bit surprised that bro. LD had him a career as a performer of some sort. Thought I recognized some kindred spirit there. I know you might have been just ramping up the theatricality when you said &#34;if that is your real name,&#34; Lost, but for the record, it be so, my name is Steve Huff. On the internet you will find a &#34;true crime&#34; blogger Steve Huff, an opera singer, a poet and publisher, a storied fishing boat captain in the Florida keys, a purveyor of porn, a legendary r&amp;b composer/producer, a lawyer, and minister or two, but I&#39;m the first two Steve Huff&#40;s&#41; - crime blogger and opera singer, and yes, that&#39;s a damned odd combination. <BR> <BR>Maybe someone should print those things out and just send &#39;em to old Denny to f&#42;&#42;k with him. I&#39;m not sure if I will, as my wife gives me odd looks enough as it is. She knows about her psychic friend of a hubby, though, and that part is probably less surprising to her than me even, since my ability to find lost things and get back on track when lost in an unfamiliar city is, I&#39;m told, family legend. <BR> <BR>But the only time I ever felt psychic before this was when, upon entering a hotel room in 1985, I sat down on the bed and looked up at my fellow choir members who were to share the room <i>&#40;we were on a tour&#41;</i> and said, without preamble, <i>&#34;there&#39;s a porn magazine under this mattress.&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>At 17 I had no idea that there&#39;s some bizarre unspoken tradition among travelling single men that they leave their whack mags under mattresses for the next inhabitant of the room. I just sat down and saw a porn magazine cover in my mind and realized there was one under me. And there it was, a Club magazine from the month prior. This was in Savannah, Georgia, by the way, the only time I&#39;ve ever visited the city before or since, even though I&#39;m now a resident of Atlanta, GA. But y&#39;all, that&#39;s tantamount to a Christopher Walken &#34;Trivial Psychic&#34; sketch from Saturday Night Live. So if I have a gift, you can see why I&#39;ve never taken it seriously.  <BR> <BR>Here&#39;s the thing, I&#39;m wondering about it, hard, because on the one hand I <b>am</b>, admittedly, interested in the paranormal. I have always been interested in the supernatural. But my core makeup is more that of a scientifically-minded skeptic. It&#39;s just a surfeit of boyish imagination that keeps my interest in high strangeness alive. Maybe it&#39;s time to page Art Bell, I don&#39;t know.  <BR> <BR>But I digressed, because what I&#39;m getting at is now I&#39;m thinking about other uncaught serials, like Zodiac. I happened on Tom&#39;s other site years ago, not long after he put it online, and though I&#39;d already read Graysmith&#39;s 1st book when it first came out in the 80&#39;s&#40;?&#41;, zodiackiller.com got my interest stoked all over again. Partly because early descriptions of the guy sounded like my Dad, with dark framed glasses. <i>&#40;My father has a distinctive physical feature that is so readily noticeable that I didn&#39;t in any way actually think it was him, not for a second - that, and the fact that under his sometimes grouchy exterior my dad&#39;s a teddy bear&#41;</i>. So I&#39;ve re-focused on Zodiac again. I also have a persistent image in my head of the killer of several people in Mons, Belgium, of all places - I call that guy the TBK - Trash Bag Killer. That&#39;s the moniker the Belgian police actually gave him. They don&#39;t use the TBK acronym in French, but obviously it&#39;s suitable in english... I keep seeing him as a character similar to Rader in some small ways - a bureacrat of some sort. Some sort of data-processing job with city government. This killer is a butcherer, like the Kingsbury Run killer from Cleveland, Ohio, the maniac who eluded Elliott Ness - TBK chops his victims into neat pieces with a saw, wraps their body parts in trash bags, and disposes of them in places with names like The River Hate, and Dead Man Street. I keep seeing TBK as a fair-haired gent, who is now middle-aged. He wears a mustache, but it&#39;s rather old-fashioned - he may even wax it. Though he&#39;s just a government functionary like Rader was, this guy is known as an eccentric because he dresses oddly, somehow. I&#39;m not sure what it is that&#39;s odd about his dress - I&#39;m thinking that he is a neat freak, like Rader was, but his way of dressing is ornate. Many who know him think he&#39;s gay, but he&#39;s not - his victims are all female. His effeminacy is a screen. He eats parts of them. He&#39;s stopped dropping them in the places with weird names because even though he liked the gothic poetry of his joke on the authorities - he&#39;s devoted to Rimbaud, Baudelaire, Poe, Verlaine as far as literature goes - he knows the Belgian system very well and knows that Belgian authorities really cracked down on their police forces a few years ago, improving training, streamlining and modernizing methodologies. He saw these implementations in action before they even made it to the press. He&#39;s still killing, and he&#39;s made dumps, but more often he uses a freezer that he keeps in his home, and dines on the victims. If TBK is ever caught, he&#39;ll come as close as any modern criminal to truly being like a Hannibal Lecter. Still, though - he&#39;s a twisted psychopath inside. He has a family, too, though I&#39;m not so sure he&#39;s still married. He will have a minor criminal record &#40;for Belgium&#41; of seemingly non-violent but sexually oriented crime. It will be old.  <BR> <BR>He also crosses the border into France. Like Andrei Chikatilo, TBK finds many victims in railway stations.  <BR> <BR>So, with stuff like that rattling in my head, I naturally want to understand why my mind takes over and even bothers, since in real life, I do have better things to do!  <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
