<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12245"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lostdog<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lostdog<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 25, 2005 - 5:44 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve, <BR> <BR>That wasn’t the  February  ‘85 issue of Club by any chance, was  it? <BR> <BR>No? <BR> <BR>Just checking...  <BR> <BR>Peach Tree Motor Inn? <BR> <BR>No? <BR> <BR>Lonely businessmen bequeathing the used porn mag... Sorry, that’s an apocryphal tale. <BR> <BR>It’s a little known fact that the Gideon Society places  a gentlemen’s magazine under the mattress in every hotel and motel room in North America. <BR> <BR>“...Steve Huff &#40;if that is your real name!&#41;...” <BR> <BR>I can’t believe I used that hackneyed line!  -- AND you busted me! <BR>Every  two-bit card worker with a ruffled tux shirt, a big medallion and a suspect hairline will use that gag while table-hopping tonight.. <BR> <BR>I am hip to the real Steve Huff  -- your blogs and your baaad asss tenor. <BR>I am a tad disappointed to learn that you are not a legendary sea captain...  <BR>but... you’re young... there’s still time matey. <BR> <BR>As for sending those posts to Prisoner D... don’t bother. <BR>He’s already read them... several times over. <BR> <BR>I have  a strong and persistent  belief that while you envisioned a killer outside your bedroom, <BR>Rader was staring through his window uncomfortably  digesting your bulls-eye post and wondering who the f&#42;&amp;% that was standing outside. <BR> <BR>You left the room  to chill out with South Park. He felt a buzzsaw chill run up his spine in South Park City. <BR> <BR>Your TBK description reminds me of Albert Finney’s magnificent Hercule Poirot  <BR>&#40;sans the fair hair&#41; from the 1974’s Murder on the Orient Express. <BR> <BR>Though, to be fair, every time I hear the word Belgian ,I think of Finney’s Poirot. <BR>So take that for what it’s worth. <BR>   <BR>LeDog <BR>   <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12245-->
